     Never white-screen, never silently destroy data someone might want recovered.
  4. [1mFirst run / missing [38;5;153mlastSeenAt[39m.[0m Defaults to [38;5;153mnow[39m, not epoch. Otherwise the first load after an upgrade dumps every
     historical crossing at once.
  5. [1mClock moved backwards.[0m [38;5;153mlastSeenAt[39m only ever advances; [38;5;153mnow < lastSeenAt[39m is clamped rather than trusted.
  6. [1mLong absence.[0m Away a month with 40 tasks overdue → all 40 show. No cap: 40 overdue tasks is real information, and
     hiding it behind "and 35 more" would be the app deciding what you care about. The panel scrolls, newest crossing
     first.
  7. [1mTwo tabs.[0m Listen for the [38;5;153mstorage[39m event and re-derive. Writes stay last-write-wins — real conflict resolution is out
     of scope and I'd rather say so than half-build it.
  8. [1mDismissed-set growth.[0m Pruned on save: IDs referencing tasks that no longer exist are dropped.
  9. [1mTimezones.[0m Store ISO-8601 UTC, render local. DST is the browser's problem, which is where it belongs.

  [1mTesting.[0m [38;5;153mnode:test[39m, no DOM:

  - [38;5;153mnotifications.test.js[39m — the heart of it, as a table: nothing due; due in future; crossed while away; crossed then
    completed; crossed then rescheduled; dismissed then reloaded; dismissed then rescheduled (must reappear); clock
    backwards.
  - [38;5;153mtasks.test.js[39m — transitions, ID stability.
  - [38;5;153mstorage.test.js[39m — round-trip, corrupt blob → backup + fresh state, version mismatch, missing fields. Needs a
    ~10-line localStorage fake.
  - [38;5;153mwatcher.test.js[39m — fake clock: arms at the nearest crossing, re-arms after firing, clamps long delays, one event per
    crossing.

  [1mStated limitation:[0m you chose unit tests only, so the UI layer — bell, panel, dismiss wiring — is verified by hand, not
  automation. The logic underneath it is fully covered; the rendering is not. That's a reasonable trade at this size,
  but it should be a known gap rather than a surprise, and I'll record it in the spec.

  [1mNon-goals[0m (written into the spec so they don't creep in): multi-user, server sync, push notifications, OS-level
  notifications, task editing, tags, priorities, sorting, search, recurring tasks, snooze.

  Does section 2 look right? If so, I'll write the spec to
  [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/2026-09-16-task-notifications-design.md[39m for your review — still no code until you've approved
  that.

